  keywords    = {race, computational social science, immigration, gender},
  abstract    = {While racial assortative mating and interracial unions have been a central interest in the study of race relations and family demography since the early twentieth century, there have been marked changes in the social contexts in which these processes have taken place in recent decades. This review article examines three important shifts: the rise of population diversity and its impact on traditional views of racial integration, the changing institution of marriage in American life, and the increasing centrality of technology in mate search and selection.},
