  keywords    = {immigration, inequality, labor-markets},
  abstract    = {This article assesses the connection between immigration and wage inequality in the United States. Departing from the focus on how the average wages of different native groups respond to immigration, the authors examine how immigrants shape the overall wage distribution. Despite evidence indicating that an increased presence of low-skilled immigrants is associated with losses at the lower end of the wage distribution, no similar result is observed between high-skilled immigrants and natives at the upper end. Instead, the presence of foreign-born workers — whether high- or low-skilled — is associated with substantial gains for high-wage natives, particularly those at the very top. Consequently, increased immigration is associated with greater wage dispersion.},
