  abstract    = {This article examines the institutional and income dynamics associated with the financialization of the U.S. economy, advancing a sociological explanation of income shifts into the finance sector. Complementary developments include banking deregulation, finance-industry concentration, the increased size and scope of institutional investors, the shareholder-value movement, and the dominance of the neoliberal policy model. The authors estimate that between 5.8 and 6.6 trillion dollars were transferred to the finance sector since 1980, and conclude that understanding inequality dynamics requires attention to market institutions and politics.},
